economic freedom is a really interesting  topic and most people i think uh they  talk about the assets they talk about  the technology but they kind of lose the  plot as to what the potential impact on  a global stage can be  what is it about the economic freedom  that you think kind of caught your  attention or really kind of drew you to  that specific idea of that measurement  yeah well you know i had experienced  this firsthand um in a couple different  ways so  one was that i had spent a year living  in buenos aires argentina i just kind of  went there after college to try to  figure out what i want to do with my  life and sort of live abroad for a bit  while i was working on a different  startup  and one of the things i saw in that  culture was just how broken um the  financial system was it was really  holding people back not only with  inflation which was kind of eroding the  wealth especially of the poorest people  in society  um but it was also making it hard to  like you know get a mortgage to buy a  house like businesses were all uh  struggling to collect and send payments  um it kind of created this atmosphere of  despair and and resignation i would say  in the culture like people just weren't  optimistic or wanting to try  try cool things to go help people  because they felt like i could all be  gone tomorrow it could all be taken away  from me so it was this like really  insidious problem in that society  and other ways i saw it was for instance  i was i was an early employee at airbnb  we were helping move money to people in  190 countries uh people renting out  their homes and um collecting payments  from people who wanted to stay there and  i just sort of had a front row seat into  how how broken the global financial  system was it was this huge barrier to  innovation it had high fees delays um  you know oftentimes it was kind of felt  like almost like corrupt or just opaque  in terms of  how it would work and it was like  it just never made sense to me growing  up with the internet where we had this  global system  you know you could send an email or a  whatsapp message it would arrive  instantly anywhere in the world for free  why weren't payments like that it's like  why was why was every single country  creating its own proprietary system that  could be corrupted or manipulated or  just have you know incompatibilities so  that's why i got excited about it and i  felt like economic freedom was this huge  unlock basically it was it was almost  like solving a meta problem because  you know you could argue things like the  industrial revolution and um some of  these things that have happened  historically  just by giving people the ability to  you know if you do something good for  the world you can capture some of the  upside that's a very simple but a very  powerful idea and arguably it's almost  sufficient to get  a huge amount of innovation and progress  happening in society so i'm very excited  about you know how do you how do you get  better more progress happening in  society how do you bet get more  technology innovation and property  rights and economic freedom are kind of  a core foundation of that so i felt like  this might be one of those rare moments  in history where a small group of people  could take this brilliant technology  innovation bitcoin  and with the  proliferation of smartphones and the  internet actually inject economic  freedom and property rights and good  financial infrastructure into all these  countries all over the world  um really even just from one company  doing that and that just i couldn't stop  thinking about it for  you know a year and so eventually i was  like alright i gotta try creating some  kind of a prototype and that led to the  origin of coinbase  uh you made a good bet because uh that  seems to be what is occurring as as  we're watching this all uh play out here  um i should say before we have a  conversation about the public company  and uh and some of the regulatory stuff  i've got equity exposure to coinbase  obviously you're a shareholder in the  business and uh nothing here is  financial advice but in terms of uh  coinbase you've built a business that is  today worth tens of billions of dollars  and recently took the company public any  lessons learned from that process of  going public any things that have  surprised you or or downsides that maybe  you didn't expect as well  um yeah you know so actually i wasn't  sure that i wanted to be a public  company ceo when i first started  coinbase because  you know i'm like an engineer product  person by background  i felt i'd read all these stories about  how once you take a company public it's  like there's kind of activist  shareholders and you have to spend you  know i talked to some public company  ceos actually and when i was trying to  figure all this out and they were some  of them told me  they spent 30 40 percent of their time  just talking with public market  investors and to me i felt like you know  this might be something that just slows  us down and how we're going to create  great products but i realized over time  that you know there's many different  ways to go public and you can actually  create this in a way that's very  beneficial to the company so  just to give you a couple examples that  are  like very positive reasons why why to do  it and why part of why we did it  you know one of them is that um it's a  huge legitimizing force for not only  your own company but for the industry uh  so for instance once we became a public  company a whole bunch of fortune 500  companies started you know being more  comfortable working with us on  especially on the institutional side of  our business and on the developer side  but it's um  i didn't realize and appreciate this  until we went public like the the level  of  legitimacy that it actually applies to  the company now on top of that it gives  a whole bunch of liquidity to the early  investors and the employees 